        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): remove abort listener on early reject paths in ls tool",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\r\n\r\n`createLsToolDefinition` registers an `onAbort` handler on the caller's `AbortSignal` at line 127 to reject the pending promise with `\"Operation aborted\"` when the signal fires. The handler is properly cleaned up in the success path (line 185) and the catch block (line 221), but three early-rejection code paths call `reject(...)` without first removing the listener:\r\n\r\n- `Path not found` (line 136)\r\n- `Not a directory` (line 143)\r\n- `Cannot read directory` (line 153)\r\n\r\n## Why This Change Was Made\r\n\r\nThe abort listener closure captures `resolve`, `reject`, `cwd`, and other local variables. When the signal never fires, the listener reference keeps this closure alive until the signal object is garbage collected. While `{ once: true }` ensures the listener self-cleans on first abort invocation, the listener leaks when the promise is rejected synchronously and the signal is never aborted.\r\n\r\nAdding `signal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort)` before each early `reject()` call matches the existing cleanup pattern already used by the success and catch paths.\r\n\r\n## User Impact\r\n\r\nNo behavioral change. The listener cleanup is a resource-management improvement — the three early-rejection paths now properly release their reference to the `AbortSignal`, matching every other exit path in the same function.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\nVerified against commit `86c47102` on branch `fix/ls-abort-listener-leak`.\r\n\r\n### Sibling pattern in `find.ts`\r\n\r\nThe sibling tool `sessions/tools/find.ts` uses a `settle()` wrapper that centralizes cleanup — every `reject()` and `resolve()` goes through `settle()`, which unconditionally removes the abort listener:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\n// find.ts:179-186 — all paths clean up via settle()\r\nlet settled = false;\r\nconst settle = (fn: () => void) => {\r\n  if (settled) return;\r\n  settled = true;\r\n  signal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\r\n  fn();\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n`ls.ts` does not have this wrapper, so each exit path is responsible for its own cleanup. The success path and catch block do it; three early-rejection paths did not.\r\n\r\n### Fix\r\n\r\nThe three added lines mirror the exact same call used at:\r\n\r\n```typescript\r\n// line 185 (success path)\r\nsignal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\r\n\r\n// line 221 (catch block)\r\nsignal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach inserted before `reject(...)` in the early-return path. The `signal?.` optional chaining is safe when `signal` is `undefined`.\r\n\r\n### Tests\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ pnpm test src/agents/agent-tools.read.arg-value-suffix.test.ts\r\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\r\nTests       3 passed (3)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Risk Checklist\r\n\r\n- **User-visible behavior:** No — zero behavioral change.\r\n- **Config/env/migration behavior:** No.\r\n- **Security/auth/secrets/network/tool execution:** No.\r\n- **Plugins/providers/channels/SDK:** No — internal to `ls` tool definition.\r\n- **Highest-risk area:** None. The change is purely additive cleanup; `removeEventListener` is a no-op when the listener has already been removed or the signal is `undefined`.\r\n- **Mitigation:** Follows the existing cleanup pattern from line 185 and line 221.\r\n\r\nCloses: N/A\r\n",
        "branch": "fix/ls-abort-listener-leak",
